{:edb/db-before-t 1554 :edb/db-after-t 1555 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534867 50 #inst "2026-10-08T09:33:08.796Z" 13194139534867 true] [17592186063962 1100 "Find the global extrema of $f(x) = 2x - x^2$  on the interval $[-1,4].$" 13194139534867 true] [17592186063962 1100 "Find the global extrema of $f(x) = 2x - x^2$ on the interval $[-1,4]$." 13194139534867 false] [17592186063962 1145 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-x^2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 2-2x\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0$, we get\n\n$$\n2-2x = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=1\n$$\n\nThe critical points are $x=1$ and the endpoints $x=-1,4$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-1) &= 2(-1) - (-1)^{2} =-3 \\\\\nf(1) &= 2(1) - (1)^{2} = 1 \\\\\nf(4) &= 2(4) - (4)^{2} =-8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2$.\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=1$, so the global maximum value is $1$.\n- The least value is $f(4)=-8$, so the global minimum value is $-8$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/364/Source/Images/a3da8fc5afb2bade8dcf669299f130d8.png)" 13194139534867 false] [17592186063962 1145 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2x-x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 2-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n2-2x = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=1.\n$$\n\nThe critical points are $x=1$ and the endpoints $x=-1,4$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)-(-1)^{2}=-3 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)-(1)^{2}=1 \\\\ f(4) & =2(4)-(4)^{2}=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=1,$ so the global maximum value is $1.$\n\n- The least value is $f(4)=-8,$ so the global minimum value is $-8.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/c277beb3423cc80d3ac2a001ac1063059b645c65fc8cb77978050f3831274b31.png)" 13194139534867 true] [17592186063963 1100 "Find the global maximum and minimum values for the function $f(x) = x^4 - 6 x^2$ on the closed interval $[-1, 3]$." 13194139534867 false] [17592186063963 1100 "Find the global maximum and minimum values for the function $f(x) = x^4 - 6 x^2$ on the closed interval $[-1, 3].$" 13194139534867 true] [17592186063963 1145 "First, we find the critical points. Taking the derivative of\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^4 - 6x^2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 4x^3 - 12x\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{3} - 12x &= 0 \\\\\n4x(x^{2} - 3) &= 0 \\\\\n4x(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - \\sqrt{3}) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, ± \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nx = -\\sqrt 3 \\approx -1.73\n$$\n\ndoes not belong to the interval $[-1,3]$, we ignore this point.\n\nSo, the critical points are\n\n$$\nx=0,\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nand the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at the critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(-1) & = (-1)^4 - 6(-1)^2 = -5 \\\\[5pt] f(0) & = (0)^4 - 6(0)^2 = 0 \\\\[5pt] f(\\sqrt 3) & = (\\sqrt 3)^4 - 6(\\sqrt 3)^2 = -9 \\\\[5pt] f(3) & = (3)^4 - 6(3)^2 = 27 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the global maximum is $27$ and the global minimum is $-9$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/364/Source/Images/80f32cf65aaa0b3f582aff95ebb1352f.png)" 13194139534867 false] [17592186063963 1145 "First, we find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x^4 - 6x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 4x^3 - 12x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-12x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-3) & =0 \\\\ 4x(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x = -\\sqrt 3 \\approx -1.73$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,3]$, we ignore this point.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,\\sqrt{3}$ and the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at the critical points:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(-1) \t& = (-1)^4 - 6(-1)^2 = -5\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(0) \t\t& = (0)^4 - 6(0)^2 = 0\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(\\sqrt 3) \t& = (\\sqrt 3)^4 - 6(\\sqrt 3)^2 = -9\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(3) \t\t& = (3)^4 - 6(3)^2 = 27 \\\t\t\n \t\t}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the global maximum is $27$ and the global minimum is $-9.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/74ce5114dbc93035b0be078be12149cced66ce881dfbc37768ed9febcdfcba87.png)" 13194139534867 true] [17592186064079 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315994 13194139534867 true] [17592186064079 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316015 13194139534867 true] [17592186064079 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316025 13194139534867 true] [17592186064079 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316031 13194139534867 true] [17592186064079 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316038 13194139534867 true] [17592186064080 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315987 13194139534867 true] [17592186064080 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316001 13194139534867 true] [17592186064080 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316008 13194139534867 true] [17592186064080 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316022 13194139534867 true] [17592186064080 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316028 13194139534867 true] [17592186315987 1017 #edb/ref 17592186315988 13194139534867 true] [17592186315987 1089 "q-20979" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315987 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534867 true] [17592186315987 1100 "For the function $f(x)=13-9{x}^{2}+2{x}^{3},$ on the interval $[-1,2],$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global maximum value is $2$\n- The global maximum value is $13$\n- The global minimum value is $-7$" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315987 1113 #uuid "cc17766e-7d7a-589d-a2b4-c4476ce1ce99" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315987 1145 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=13-9{x}^{2}+2{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-18x+6{x}^{2} \\\\  & =6x(x-3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n6x(x-3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\,3.\n$$\n\nSince $x=3$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,2]$, the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =13-9(-1)^{2}+2(-1)^{3}=2 \\\\ f(0) & =13-9(0)^{2}+2(0)^{3}=13 \\\\ f(2) & =13-9(2)^{2}+2(2)^{3}=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(0)=13,$ so the global maximum is $13.$\n\n- The least value is $f(2)=-7,$ so the global minimum is $-7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/1a9d2f0db241c0a209c315a8e901e40a44258f339bbaa7bee36198b58fe2684d.png)" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315988 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315989 13194139534867 true] [17592186315988 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315990 13194139534867 true] [17592186315988 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315991 13194139534867 true] [17592186315988 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315992 13194139534867 true] [17592186315988 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315993 13194139534867 true] [17592186315988 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534867 true] [17592186315988 1041 "selection" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315988 1104 #edb/ref 17592186315989 13194139534867 true] [17592186315988 1110 #uuid "80c3f044-20fe-5b63-8169-ac0b5497f977" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315989 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315989 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534867 true] [17592186315989 1111 #uuid "2dc8ae58-8f87-5d5d-a1bd-5fb0ba2825b1" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315990 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315990 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534867 true] [17592186315990 1111 #uuid "2959f46d-b800-56a5-9515-0c8c1497d824" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315991 1034 "I only" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315991 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534867 true] [17592186315991 1111 #uuid "f13b449b-9ebd-5af0-9df7-ea4310b7b0c9" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315992 1034 "III only" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315992 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534867 true] [17592186315992 1111 #uuid "4821b667-c753-576a-8c9d-6f20be5398fb" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315993 1034 "II only" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315993 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534867 true] [17592186315993 1111 #uuid "734b9d6f-edd4-52f0-b3ab-e5ff49d52a67" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315994 1017 #edb/ref 17592186315995 13194139534867 true] [17592186315994 1089 "q-22568" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315994 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534867 true] [17592186315994 1100 "For the function $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x},$ defined on the interval $[\\frac{1}{2},2],$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global maximum value is $\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}$\n- The global minimum value is $2\\sqrt{e}$\n- The global minimum value is $4\\sqrt[4]{e}$" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315994 1113 #uuid "ee14f683-6384-519d-813e-983505d2615a" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315994 1145 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot x-{e}^{x}\\cdot 1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(x-1)}{{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{e}^{x}(x-1)}{{x}^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=1,$ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{{e}^{1/2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2\\sqrt{e}≈3.3 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{{e}^{1}}{1}=e≈2.7 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}≈3.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(2)=\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(1)=e,$ so the global minimum is $e.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/98156f05b9b6c71c7e5a5753d024d0851a0a779741545ba2d62d3d63af41e824.png)" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315995 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315996 13194139534867 true] [17592186315995 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315997 13194139534867 true] [17592186315995 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315998 13194139534867 true] [17592186315995 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315999 13194139534867 true] [17592186315995 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316000 13194139534867 true] [17592186315995 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534867 true] [17592186315995 1041 "selection" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315995 1104 #edb/ref 17592186315997 13194139534867 true] [17592186315995 1110 #uuid "e19263a6-16c3-5855-a3c8-7f1d598aee2e" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315996 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315996 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534867 true] [17592186315996 1111 #uuid "cc54b5d4-de6d-5666-b16a-5b5f9eebec60" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315997 1034 "I only" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315997 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534867 true] [17592186315997 1111 #uuid "c594a10b-eeb0-5295-99aa-e8a0d0fb27be" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315998 1034 "III only" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315998 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534867 true] [17592186315998 1111 #uuid "bff49c6e-7efe-563e-bd1c-d269c794a541" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315999 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534867 true] [17592186315999 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534867 true] [17592186315999 1111 #uuid "90c428bc-0a29-5ff9-a8f4-bda4a4a565b8" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316000 1034 "II only" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316000 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534867 true] [17592186316000 1111 #uuid "63ef7540-b3a5-5756-84a9-22ff1cb7f0ad" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316001 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316002 13194139534867 true] [17592186316001 1089 "q-2690" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316001 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534867 true] [17592186316001 1100 "For the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+9x+3,$ on the closed interval $-4\\le x\\le -2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global maximum value is $3$\n- The global minimum value is $1$\n- The global minimum value is $-1$" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316001 1113 #uuid "a76d75de-a43f-5f31-a4df-679658344b30" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316001 1145 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+9x+3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+12x+9 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+4x+3) \\\\  & =3(x+1)(x+3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n3(x+1)(x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,\\,-1.\n$$\n\nSince $x=-1$ does not belong to the interval $[-4,-2]$, the critical points are $x=-3$ and the endpoints $x=-4,-2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =(-4)^{3}+6(-4)^{2}+9(-4)+3=-1 \\\\ f(-3) & =(-3)^{3}+6(-3)^{2}+9(-3)+3=3 \\\\ f(-2) & =(-2)^{3}+6(-2)^{2}+9(-2)+3=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-3)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The least value is $f(-4)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/fa85119166ca325f9925ddd03a578fb5daae463c59c3b7b9f7ac0f6464a30241.png)" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316002 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316003 13194139534867 true] [17592186316002 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316004 13194139534867 true] [17592186316002 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316005 13194139534867 true] [17592186316002 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316006 13194139534867 true] [17592186316002 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316007 13194139534867 true] [17592186316002 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534867 true] [17592186316002 1041 "selection" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316002 1104 #edb/ref 17592186316003 13194139534867 true] [17592186316002 1110 #uuid "33c88080-7cfe-5e38-87d0-3c8e9d3ce18b" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316003 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316003 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534867 true] [17592186316003 1111 #uuid "eccddfc2-787b-5516-b6bd-30ee103cdc1f" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316004 1034 "I only" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316004 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534867 true] [17592186316004 1111 #uuid "e86999c2-844c-5409-a5d4-6786f597cab7" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316005 1034 "III only" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316005 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534867 true] [17592186316005 1111 #uuid "0f5b8aa3-0ebf-5b70-ba09-96f603a67e31" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316006 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316006 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534867 true] [17592186316006 1111 #uuid "4cfb02b0-e52c-5343-963c-7b698f3e6dae" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316007 1034 "II only" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316007 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534867 true] [17592186316007 1111 #uuid "f071f8b6-9e53-5967-9de9-8ed3725496b7" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316008 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316009 13194139534867 true] [17592186316008 1089 "q-2721" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316008 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534867 true] [17592186316008 1100 "Find  the global minimum value of the function $f(x)=3{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+3$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 1.$" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316008 1113 #uuid "75ad9ded-37c4-58ab-9d8e-190ad31a442b" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316008 1145 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=3{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 12{x}^{2}(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,1]$, the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =3(-1)^{4}-8(-1)^{3}+3=14 \\\\ f(0) & =3(0)^{4}-8(0)^{3}+3=3 \\\\ f(1) & =3(1)^{4}-8(1)^{3}+3=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=14,$ so the global maximum is $14.$\n\n- The least value is $f(1)=-2,$ so the global minimum is $-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/9ac7f0c0e28532c897e1c89a35e9c027f614fee40b4b94362a6c5cb012c8a7f6.png)" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316009 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316010 13194139534867 true] [17592186316009 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316011 13194139534867 true] [17592186316009 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316012 13194139534867 true] [17592186316009 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316013 13194139534867 true] [17592186316009 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316014 13194139534867 true] [17592186316009 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534867 true] [17592186316009 1041 "selection" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316009 1104 #edb/ref 17592186316014 13194139534867 true] [17592186316009 1110 #uuid "e1fb84fa-fc95-53e7-ab91-60395ae2e860" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316010 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316010 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534867 true] [17592186316010 1111 #uuid "f866ee17-3f63-5d28-8f45-57a706dbdf99" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316011 1034 "1" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316011 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534867 true] [17592186316011 1111 #uuid "135002de-959d-5cef-8f60-e7350d241680" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316012 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316012 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534867 true] [17592186316012 1111 #uuid "e06450b7-a2b9-56e3-934f-401225d3a340" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316013 1034 "0" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316013 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534867 true] [17592186316013 1111 #uuid "c59df80f-343b-5b16-ba93-d00477917cdb" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316014 1034 "-2" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316014 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534867 true] [17592186316014 1111 #uuid "292d2c46-5c4d-58f1-a16e-4efd101bed05" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316015 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316016 13194139534867 true] [17592186316015 1089 "q-2751" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316015 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534867 true] [17592186316015 1100 "Find the global maximum value of the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ on the closed interval $4\\le x\\le 9.$" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316015 1113 #uuid "5b45f1eb-150e-598c-9260-0677325c6011" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316015 1145 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x>0.$ So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=4,9$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ f(9) & =\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(9)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The least value is $f(4)=2,$ so the global minimum is $2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/c8badccb8144447b5b57157308b5614293a54569f8247320a358dd20650d91a1.png)" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316016 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316017 13194139534867 true] [17592186316016 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316018 13194139534867 true] [17592186316016 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316019 13194139534867 true] [17592186316016 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316020 13194139534867 true] [17592186316016 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316021 13194139534867 true] [17592186316016 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534867 true] [17592186316016 1041 "selection" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316016 1104 #edb/ref 17592186316019 13194139534867 true] [17592186316016 1110 #uuid "1b9a9e16-fdb0-55e1-a4a2-98937811b39a" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316017 1034 "\\frac{7}{2}" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316017 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534867 true] [17592186316017 1111 #uuid "771c151a-b5d8-5fac-920c-833d11cd9d1d" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316018 1034 "\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316018 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534867 true] [17592186316018 1111 #uuid "39bf8cf4-631f-503e-8f68-231b91db7827" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316019 1034 "3" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316019 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534867 true] [17592186316019 1111 #uuid "34764b05-8c0d-5b8b-9602-b7098444fb24" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316020 1034 "2" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316020 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534867 true] [17592186316020 1111 #uuid "5ddb721e-2c0b-5710-b875-b8743c216d54" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316021 1034 "4" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316021 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534867 true] [17592186316021 1111 #uuid "b0fd0dd6-6600-5efe-a99c-d6274ebe5a49" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316022 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316023 13194139534867 true] [17592186316022 1089 "q-314729" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316022 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534867 true] [17592186316022 1100 "The exact global minimum value of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+1$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 1$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139534867 true] [17592186316022 1113 #uuid "9641cbff-6df9-5810-96c4-dcfb71465c80" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316022 1145 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =8{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}(2x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,1],$ the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)^{4}-4(-1)^{3}+1=2+4+1=7 \\\\ f(0) & =2(0)^{4}-4(0)^{3}+1=1 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)^{4}-4(1)^{3}+1=2-4+1=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=7,$ so the global maximum is $7.$\n\n- The least value is $f(1)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/fa4a3f723bb55521ec02dda8d088427b1939721366155f5ac45dd8e1429992e7.png)" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316023 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316024 13194139534867 true] [17592186316023 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534867 true] [17592186316023 1041 "field-1" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316023 1104 #edb/ref 17592186316024 13194139534867 true] [17592186316023 1110 #uuid "c3729fc4-3797-5340-81aa-57fda3329d71" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316024 1034 "-1" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316024 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534867 true] [17592186316024 1111 #uuid "25d86cfd-3823-5e43-a83b-0971d6221498" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316025 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316026 13194139534867 true] [17592186316025 1089 "q-344373" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316025 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534867 true] [17592186316025 1100 "The global maximum value of the function $f(x)=-x+4$ on the closed interval $1\\le x\\le 5$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139534867 true] [17592186316025 1113 #uuid "19474498-643c-582f-bfde-d7a62f0ea3d2" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316025 1145 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-x+4,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all real values of $x.$ So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=1,5$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-(1)+4=3 \\\\ f(5) & =-(5)+4=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(5)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/b66a275ad0f733f9b7a715cacc87e47ab42200def67a3a7f5a5e577ba067a1c3.png)" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316026 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316027 13194139534867 true] [17592186316026 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534867 true] [17592186316026 1041 "field-1" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316026 1104 #edb/ref 17592186316027 13194139534867 true] [17592186316026 1110 #uuid "eb070f25-1f33-5ae9-88b9-f858c9562c5b" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316027 1034 "3" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316027 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534867 true] [17592186316027 1111 #uuid "627ff948-bc36-53f5-b9dd-f6d03140cb38" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316028 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316029 13194139534867 true] [17592186316028 1089 "q-344443" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316028 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534867 true] [17592186316028 1100 "The global minimum value of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le 2\\pi $ is{{field-1}}." 13194139534867 true] [17592186316028 1113 #uuid "8f3a3f30-2b26-5f4e-9197-abe610a5701d" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316028 1145 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ since they are the only values that belongs to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ and the endpoints $x=0,2\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0 \\\\ f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1 \\\\ f\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =sin\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ f(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1,$ so the global maximum is $1.$\n\n- The least value is $f\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/c5368ebe232e9ad044cacb16300fb2f3f1747fefd326f4be4e0c22d0e3a7069d.png)" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316030 13194139534867 true] [17592186316029 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534867 true] [17592186316029 1041 "field-1" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316029 1104 #edb/ref 17592186316030 13194139534867 true] [17592186316029 1110 #uuid "450ece59-a01c-5d2b-8d21-5b65bc0915b7" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316030 1034 "-1" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316030 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534867 true] [17592186316030 1111 #uuid "4e2aef30-9cde-5935-a9a7-3337f99cffaf" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316031 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316032 13194139534867 true] [17592186316031 1089 "q-79342" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316031 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534867 true] [17592186316031 1100 "Find the global minimum value of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1$ on the closed interval $[-1,3].$" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316031 1113 #uuid "941af544-ae4c-5a01-8911-5ecc21b5700c" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316031 1145 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-3{x}^{2}+6x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}+6x & =0 \\\\ -3x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,2,$ and the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}-1=3 \\\\ f(0) & =-(0)^{3}+3(0)^{2}-1=-1 \\\\ f(2) & =-(2)^{3}+3(2)^{2}-1=3 \\\\ f(3) & =-(3)^{3}+3(3)^{2}-1=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=f(2)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(0)=f(3)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/ebc8acabf87ca5c3423f2f7c07637a87c5963d9cb080a4d52f9efd87846380d1.png)" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316033 13194139534867 true] [17592186316032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316034 13194139534867 true] [17592186316032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316035 13194139534867 true] [17592186316032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316036 13194139534867 true] [17592186316032 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316037 13194139534867 true] [17592186316032 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534867 true] [17592186316032 1041 "selection" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316032 1104 #edb/ref 17592186316037 13194139534867 true] [17592186316032 1110 #uuid "7c9a0c81-1e18-52e2-b8f7-b3422105b226" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316033 1034 "-5" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316033 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534867 true] [17592186316033 1111 #uuid "fb7c8a88-7f04-5f7e-9b7f-f4f5587b5967" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316034 1034 "3" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316034 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534867 true] [17592186316034 1111 #uuid "ca196cc7-5247-57ff-aab0-7f32005b1f39" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316035 1034 "2" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316035 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534867 true] [17592186316035 1111 #uuid "c7c2d143-9fbc-55ff-b6d3-fb85b27cdf06" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316036 1034 "0" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316036 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534867 true] [17592186316036 1111 #uuid "12b0d977-b3cb-5278-8cd6-1f04b91f46cc" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316037 1034 "-1" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316037 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534867 true] [17592186316037 1111 #uuid "96a1a329-9bb4-5188-ae5d-6b9297be6feb" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316038 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316039 13194139534867 true] [17592186316038 1089 "q-79348" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316038 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534867 true] [17592186316038 1100 "For the function $f(x)=-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ defined on the closed interval $[-2,1],$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global minimum value is $-1$\n- The global maximum value is $1$\n- The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}$" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316038 1113 #uuid "31376917-58ed-53d3-897e-f420b4febe17" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316038 1145 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1({x}^{2}+1)-x(2x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\pm 1,$ and the endpoints $x=-2,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{(-2)^{2}+1}=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ f(-1) & =-\\frac{(-1)}{(-1)^{2}+1}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(1) & =-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(1)=-\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global minimum is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042485/assets/f3165b8b46c8ac0ab798ae6162d4b4facb92367f5072598bef936801bce44fc4.png)" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316039 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316040 13194139534867 true] [17592186316039 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316041 13194139534867 true] [17592186316039 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316042 13194139534867 true] [17592186316039 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316043 13194139534867 true] [17592186316039 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316044 13194139534867 true] [17592186316039 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534867 true] [17592186316039 1041 "selection" 13194139534867 true] [17592186316039 1104 #edb/ref 17592186316042 13194139534867 true] [17592186316039 1110 #uuid "200ef30a-708e-5701-8caa-a9